    "created_at": "2017-10-13T19:05:34Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-10-13T19:05:34Z",
    "body": "> (That said, it's not clear to me why we'd use the base URI for import() in a script, but not for other URL resolutions. Is there a principled reason for that?)\r\n\r\nThe reasoning is that import() is a syntactic form, and thus can be sensitive to the context (i.e. file) in which it appears. Whereas, a function like setTimeout() cannot be. (Or at least should not be; it shouldn't do stack inspection, even if it's theoretically possible.)\r\n\r\n> I'm fine with aligning them.\r\n\r\nGreat!\r\n\r\n> With regard to the nonce and parser metadata, I think the reset is reasonable.\r\n\r\nHmm, now I'm confused. Should we align them and let them inherit, like eval() does? Or should we reset them?\r\n\r\n> so we shouldn't treat it as though it has a nonce\r\n\r\nWell, we currently treat all static `import` statements as though they have a nonce: otherwise you wouldn't be able to import any scripts. We also treat all dynamic imports inside eval() as though they have a nonce, because the JS spec doesn't really distinguish between eval()ed code and normal code in this way. (They are not \"separate scripts\".)\r\n\r\nConcretely, given a policy like `script-src 'nonce-correct' 'unsafe-eval'`,\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<script nonce=\"correct\">\r\neval(`import(\"./x\")`); // succeeds\r\nsetTimeout(`import(\"./y\")`); // fails\r\n```\r\n\r\nIt'd be more consistent to treat setTimeout() as though it has the nonce, i.e. inherits it.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
