    "body": "> The reasoning is that import() is a syntactic form, and thus can be sensitive to the context (i.e. file) in which it appears. Whereas, a function like setTimeout() cannot be. (Or at least should not be; it shouldn't do stack inspection, even if it's theoretically possible.)\r\n\r\nI think I agree with @annevk's implication that it's not clear that `import()`'s behavior makes sense. That said, I am not a JavaScript language person, so I'm happy to just throw my opinion out there, and defer to experts on conclusions.\r\n\r\n> Concretely, given a policy like script-src 'nonce-correct' 'unsafe-eval',\r\n>\r\n> ```\r\n> <script nonce=\"correct\">\r\n> eval(`import(\"./x\")`); // succeeds\r\n> setTimeout(`import(\"./y\")`); // fails\r\n> ```\r\n\r\n1.  It surprises me that these are different.\r\n2.  As I noted above, I agree that \"Well, we currently treat all static import statements as though they have a nonce: otherwise you wouldn't be able to import any scripts.\", but I think that means we need to define an import mechanism that accepts nonces, not that we should treat these mechanisms as though they were nonced.\r\n\r\n@arturjanc, who probably has opinions since he's actually deploying nonces in the wild (though his use-cases are generally in the presence of `'strict-dynamic'`, which changes the calculus a bit).",
